In the previous video, you saw how you can use vectorization to compute the predictions,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.800
the lowercase a's, for an entire training set all sort of at the same time.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.500
In this video, you see how you can use vectorization to also perform the gradient computations for

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.640
all m training samples, again, all sort of at the same time.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.680
And then at the end of this video, we'll pull it all together and show how you can

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.880
derive a very efficient implementation of logistic regression.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.440
So you will remember that for the gradient computation, what we did was we computed dz1

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:49.480
for the first example, which is going to be a1 minus y1, and then dz2 equals a2 minus y2,

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.360
and so on.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.520
And so on for all m training samples.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:02.080
So what we're going to do is define a new variable d capital Z, which is going to be

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.560
dz1, dz2, dzm.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:17.200
Again, all the d lowercase z variables stacked horizontally, so this would be a 1 by m matrix,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.280
or alternatively, a m dimensional row vector.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.840
Now recall that from the previous slide, we'd already figured out how to compute capital

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.400
A, which was this a1 through am.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:39.520
And we had defined capital Y as y1 through ym, also, you know, stacked horizontally.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.640
So based on these definitions, maybe you can see for yourself that dz can be computed as

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:54.320
just a minus y, because this is going to be equal to a1 minus y1, excuse me, first element,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.200
a2 minus y2, second element, and so on.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:07.120
And so this first element, a1 minus y1 is exactly the definition of dz1, the second element is

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.100
exactly the definition of dz2, and so on.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:20.160
So with just one line of code, you can compute all of this at the same time.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:26.480
Now in the previous implementation, we've gotten rid of one for loop already, but we

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.680
still had this second for loop over training examples.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.560
So we initialize dw to zero, to a vector of zeros, but then we still had to loop over

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:45.200
training examples where we have dw plus equals x1 times dz1 for the first training example,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.480
dw plus equals x2, dz2, and so on.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.120
So we do the m times and then dw divide equals by m.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:04.000
And similarly for b, right, db was initialized as zero, and then db plus equals dz1, db

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:13.280
plus equals dz2, down to, you know, dzm, and then db divide equals m.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.040
So that's what we had in the previous implementation.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.760
We'd already gotten rid of one for loop, so at least now dw was a vector and we weren't

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.920
separately updating dw1, dw2, and so on.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:32.240
So we've gotten rid of that already, but we still had a for loop over the m examples

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.720
in the training set.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.840
So let's take these operations and vectorize them.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.320
Here's what we can do.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.200
For the vectorized implementation of db, what it's doing is basically summing up all

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.200
of these dz's and then dividing by m.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:57.200
So db is basically 1 over m, summed from i equals 1 through m of dzi, and where all

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.520
the dz's are in that row vector.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:12.400
And so in Python, what you do is implement 1 over m times np dot sum of dz.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.160
So just take this variable and call the np dot sum function on it, and that will give

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.000
you db.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.000
How about dw?

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.760
I'll just write out the correct equations.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:26.680
You can verify it's the right thing to do.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:34.920
So dw turns out to be 1 over m times the matrix x times dz transpose.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:41.680
And to kind of see why that's the case, this is equal to 1 over m, then the matrix x is

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.760
x1 through xm, stacked up in columns like that.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:56.520
And dz transpose is going to be dz1 down to dzm like so.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.520
And so if you figure out what this matrix times this vector works out to be, this turns out

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:14.720
to be 1 over m times x1 dz1 plus dot dot dot plus xm dzm.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:22.560
And so this is a n by 1 vector, and this is what you actually end up with, with dw, because

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.520
dw was taking these xi, dzi, and adding them up.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.560
And so that's what exactly this matrix vector multiplication is doing.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.080
And so again, with one line of code, you can compute dw.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.960
So the vectorized implementation of the derivative calculations is just this.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:47.400
You use this line to implement db, and use this line to implement dw.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.600
And notice that without a for loop over the training set, you can now compute the

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.680
updates you want to your parameters.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.280
So now let's put it all together into how you would actually implement logistic regression.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.320
So this is our original highly inefficient non-vectorized implementation, where, so the

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.880
first thing we've done in the previous video was get rid of this volume, right?

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:18.440
So instead of looping over dw1, dw2, and so on, we have replaced this with a vector

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:25.960
that's a value dw, which I say this is dw plus equals xi, which is now a vector, times

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.160
dzi.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:34.600
But now we'll see that we can also get rid of not just the for loop below, but also get

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.800
rid of this for loop.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.080
So here's how you do it.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:48.420
So using what we had from the previous slides, you would say capital Z is equal to w trans

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.220
post x plus b.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:07:02.180
And code you write is capital Z equals NP dot w transpose x plus b.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:07.460
And then a equals sigmoid of capital Z.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:12.780
So you've now computed all of this and all of this for all the values of i.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:21.140
Next on the previous slide, we said you would compute dz equals capital A minus capital Y.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:25.020
So now you've computed all of this for all the values of i.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:34.900
Then finally, dw equals 1 over m x dz transpose.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:45.460
And db equals 1 over m of NP dot sum dz.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:50.980
So you've just done for propagation and back propagation, really computing the predictions

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:57.740
and computing the derivatives on all m training examples without using a for loop.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:03.420
And so the gradient descent update then would be w gets updated as w minus the learning

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.460
rate times dw, which you just computed above.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:12.940
And b gets updated as b minus the learning rate times db.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:17.940
Sometimes it's putting colon z to denote this as an assignment.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.700
But I guess I haven't been totally consistent with that notation.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:28.420
But with this, you've just implemented a single iteration of gradient descent for logistic

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:29.420
regression.

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:34.580
Now, I know I said that we should get rid of explicit for loops whenever you can.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:40.780
But if you want to implement multiple iterations of gradient descent, then you still need a

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.980
for loop over the number of iterations.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:49.420
So if you want to have a thousand iterations of gradient descent, you might still need

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:55.700
a for loop over the iteration number as an outermost for loop like that.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.860
And I don't think there's any way to get rid of that for loop.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:03.740
But I do think it's incredibly cool that you can implement at least one iteration of

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:07.420
gradient descent without needing to use a for loop.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:08.420
So that's it.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.900
You now have a highly vectorized and highly efficient implementation of gradient descent

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.900
for logistic regression.

